"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[5407],{5407:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"minor-subjects-test-2","slug":"minor-subjects","subject":"Minor Subjects","testNo":2,"durationMin":60,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient is undergoing a pre-anesthetic evaluation, and an image of their mouth is provided. Based on the modified Mallampati score, which class does this patient fall into?","options":["Class IV","Class III","Class II","Class I"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Class I\\n\\nIn the given image, the faucial pillars, uvula, and the soft palate are all visible, so it is classified as class I according to the modified Mallampati score.\\n\\nThe modified Mallampati score is used to assess the difficulty of endotracheal intubation in a patient.\\n\\nThe patient is seated upright with the neutral position of the head, open mouth, and protruded tongue and the patient is asked to not speak. The classification is done based on the visibility of structures through the mouth in this position. The\xa0Mallampati classification modified by Samsoon and Young is as follows:\\n\\n• Class I: Faucial pillars, uvula, and soft palate are visualized.\\n• Class II: The base of the uvula, fauces and soft palate are visualized (Option c).\\n• Class III: Only the soft palate and base of uvula are visualized (Option b).\\n• Class IV: Only the Hard palate is visualized(Option a).\\n\\nHigher scores on the Mallampati classification indicate a more difficult laryngoscopy and subsequent intubation.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/ad2d5a29ae57d0be85cd.png"]},{"n":2,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Resistance of the tube shown below is primarily because of :","options":["Diameter","Curvature","Circumference","Length"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Diameter\\n\\nThe airway device shown in the image is an endotracheal tube and the resistance to the airflow through it primarily depends on diameter(Internal diameter).\\n\\n• The size of an endotracheal tube usually refers to the\xa0internal diameter in mm. Rarely the French scale is used where the size corresponds to the external diameter multiplied by 3.\\n• For adults, usually, a 7-mm is used for women and a 7.5 or 8-mm ETT is used for men.\\n• Generally, high-volume, low-pressure cuffed tubes are used in adults, and cuffless tubes are used in neonates and infants.\\n• Besides age, the choice of an endotracheal tube also depends on the presence of airway pathologies and the reason behind the placement of the tube.\\n• Smaller-sized tubes lead to increase airway resistance and increased work breathing.\\n• Larger tubes have a higher risk of laryngeal and tracheal trauma and sore throat.\\n\\nAge\\nInternal diameter (mm)\\nLength (cm)\\n\\nFull term infant\\n3.5\\n12\\n\\nChild\\n4\xa0\xa0+\xa0(Age/4)\\n12\xa0+ (Age/2)\\n\\nAdult female\\n7.0–7.5\\n\\n20-22\\n\\nAdult male\\n7.5–9.0\\n22-24","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/e3cfe1677548ab5eb318.jpg"]},{"n":3,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 12-year-old boy with academic and behavioral problems at school over the past year was diagnosed with attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder. Which of the following neurotransmitters plays a key role in the pathophysiology of this condition?","options":["Serotonin","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Gamma-amino butyric acid (GABA)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dopamine\\n\\nDopamine has been hypothesized as the key neurotransmitter involved in the pathophysiology of attention-deficit hyperactive disorder.\\n\\nThe prefrontal cortex of the brain has been implicated in Attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) because of its high utilization of dopamine and its reciprocal connections with other brain regions involved in attention, inhibition, decision-making, response inhibition, working memory, and vigilance. Apart from dopamine, imbalances in the levels of norepinephrine also play a role in this disorder.\\n\\nAttention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neuropsychiatric condition affecting children, adolescents, and adults around the world, characterized by a pattern of diminished sustained attention, and increased impulsivity or hyperactivity. They often have significant impairment in academic functioning as well as in social and interpersonal situations.\\n\\nThe clinical features of ADHD are :\\n\\n• Inattention- difficulty focusing, distractibility, not listening or following instructions, failure to finish tasks, forgetfulness, losing/misplacing objects.\\n• Hyperactivity-\xa0fidgety, unable to sit still, as though driven by a motor, hyper-talkative, interrupts, blurts out answers.\\n• Impulsivity-\xa0action before thought, abrupt shifts in activity, lack of organization, jumping up in class.\\n• Other symptoms may include memory and thinking deficits and specific learning disabilities.\\n\\nA variety of medications and behavioral interventions are used to treat ADHD. The most widely used medications are methylphenidate, d-amphetamine, and other amphetamines. These drugs are stimulants that affect the level of the neurotransmitter dopamine at the synapse. Interventions used to treat ADHD include several forms of psychotherapy, such as cognitive-behavioral therapy, social skills training, support groups, and parent and educator skills training. A combination of medication and psychotherapy is more effective than either treatment alone.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption A. Serotonin is involved in the pathophysiology of conditions like depression, mania, schizophrenia, and anxiety.\\n\\nOption B. Acetylcholine\xa0levels are implicated in diseases like Parkinson\'s disease, Alzheimer\'s disease, and Huntington\'s disease.\\n\\nOption D. Gamma amino butyric acid (GABA)\xa0level fluctuations are seen in diseases like schizophrenia, Huntington\'s disease, and various forms of epilepsy.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient with carcinoma endometrium is treated with pelvic external beam irradiation. Which of the following organs is likely to be affected the most?","options":["Vagina","Bladder","Rectum","Ovary"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Ovary\\n\\nAmong the given options, the ovary is most likely to be affected as it is the most radiosensitive organ.\\n\\nRadiosensitivity of pelvic organs is as follows:\xa0ovary>rectum>bladder>vagina.\xa0Radiation tolerance dose for ovary is 2-3 Gy whereas vagina 90 Gy. Hence, among the given options, the vagina is the most radioresistant organ.\\n\\nThe radiation dose necessary to induce ovarian failure is age-dependent. A single dose of 3-4 Gray can induce amenorrhea in almost all women >40 yrs of age. In young women, oogenesis is much less sensitive to radiation as compared to the elderly.\\n\\nRadiosensitivity of various tissues is summed up in the table given below:\\n\\nMost radiosensitive\\nLeast radiosensitive\\n\\nTissue\\nBone marrow, Gonads\\nNervous tissue, Muscle fibers\\n\\nBlood cells\\nLymphocytes\\nPlatelets\\n\\nStages of cell cycle\\nG2M interphase\\nS phase\\n\\nCell types\\nRapidly dividing\\nQuiescent\\n\\nOcular structure\\nLens\\nSclera\\n\\nLayer of retina\\nRods\\nGanglion cell layer\\n\\nThe skin is most commonly affected due to radiation. Stratum basalis is the layer of the skin most affected.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 25-year-old man with a history of schizophrenia was brought to the hospital with acute onset of neck stiffness. On examination, his upper body and neck are rigid. On review of his medications, it is seen that he was started on haloperidol 5 days back. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat his symptoms?","options":["Benztropine","Fluphenazine","Rasagiline","Amitryptiline"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Benztropine\\n\\nBased on the clinical presentation and the history of recent haloperidol usage the most probable diagnosis is acute dystonia.\xa0\xa0Treatment for this condition includes anticholinergics, such as benztropine, diphenhydramine, or trihexyphenidyl.\\n\\nClassification of antipsychotic drugs:\\n\\nGeneration\\nD2 receptor\\n5HT2A antagonism\\nPathways\\nDrugs\\n\\nFirst\xa0(Typical)\\nPreferred\\nLow\\nAll of them- mesocortical, mesolimbic, nigrostriatal, tuberoinfundibular, and CTZ\\n\\nHaloperidol, Fluphenazine, Chlorpromazine,\\n\\nThioridazine (least D2 blocking among typical, markedly anticholinergic)\\n\\nSecond\xa0(Atypical)\\nSelective\\n\\nHigh\\n\\nMostly mesolimbic\xa0and mesocortical\\n\\nRisperidone, Sertindole, Aripiprazole, Quetiapine, Clozapine","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient stopped alcohol consumption for 3 days and presented with irritability, disorientation, paranoid delusions, agitation, visual hallucinations, and altered sensorium. What is the likely diagnosis in this case?","options":["Delirium tremens","Wernicke\'s encephalopathy","Korsakoff psychosis","Alcohol-induced psychosis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Delirium tremens\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a patient who has not consumed alcohol for the past 3 days presenting with disorientation, paranoid delusions, visual hallucinations, and altered sensorium is suggestive of delirium tremens.\\n\\nDelirium tremens is a medical emergency that occurs during alcohol withdrawal. It is an acute condition and usually develops within 72 hours after the last drink. It has symptoms of both delirium and alcohol withdrawal.\\n\\nThe clinical features of delirium tremens are as follows:\\n\\n• Altered sensorium with disorientation\\n• Perceptual distortions, most frequently tactile or visual hallucinations\\n• Autonomic hyperactivity with tachycardia, fever, anxiety, insomnia, hypertension, and sweating\\n• Fluctuating levels of psychomotor activity, ranging from agitation to lethargy\\n• Altered sleep-wake cycle\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Wernicke’s encephalopathy (alcoholic encephalopathy)\xa0is associated with\xa0thiamine deficiency. Alcohol abuse may lead to thiamine deficiency because of poor nutritional habits or malabsorption.\\n\\nIt is an acute neurological disorder characterized by\\n\\n• Global confusion\\n• Ocular abnormalities like horizontal nystagmus, gaze palsy, anisocoria, and sluggish pupillary reflex\\n• Ataxia - mainly affecting the gait\\n• Vestibular dysfunction\\n\\nIt may resolve with thiamine therapy or progress to Korsakoff\'s syndrome.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Korsakoff’s psychosis can follow Wernicke’s encephalopathy due to thiamine deficiency. It is a chronic amnestic syndrome that is characterized by the following clinical symptoms in an intact and responsive patient:\\n\\n• Impaired mental syndrome, especially recent memory\\n• Anterograde amnesia\\n• Confabulation\\n\\nOption D: Alcohol-induced psychosis is characterized by the development of auditory hallucinations or paranoid delusions in the context of heavy drinking or alcohol withdrawal. These patients have a clear sensorium, unlike the patients having delirium tremens.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient presented with complaints of recurrent patellar dislocation. Which of the following would not be a cause for this condition?","options":["Patella baja","Patella alta","Small patella","Excessive joint laxity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Patella baja\\n\\nPatella baja is an abnormally low-lying patella and is not responsible for recurrent dislocation of the patella.\\n\\nCauses for recurrent dislocation of the patella include:\\n\\n• Excessive joint laxity as in connective tissue disorders like EDS, Marfan\'s syndrome\\n• Patella\xa0alta - the patella is high-lying in the shallow part of the inter-condylar groove\\n• Trochlear dysplasia\\n• Small patella\\n• Genu valgum\\n\\nGiven below is a lateral radiograph of the knee joint showing the Insall-Salvati index. This is the\xa0ratio\xa0of the\xa0patella tendon length (LT)\xa0to the\xa0length of the patella (LP).\\n\\n• In patients with\xa0high-lying\xa0patella,\xa0the ratio is\xa0above 1.2. This is known as\xa0patella alta.\\n• In patients with\xa0low-lying\xa0patella, the ratio is\xa0below 0.8.\xa0This is known as\xa0patella baja.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b57291062558e1d84c51.jpg"}]},{"n":8,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 48-year-old man was on crutches for a few months after a road traffic accident. After the initial 2 weeks, he complained of an inability to extend his wrist. He achieved complete recovery a few weeks later. What is the mechanism behind this phenomenon?","options":["Neurotmesis","Axonotmesis","Neurolysis","Neurapraxia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Neurapraxia\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a man not able to extend his wrist after the use of a crutch and complete recovery after a few weeks is suggestive of crutch palsy which is a type of neurapraxia.\\n\\nNeurapraxia is a reversible physiological block in nerve conduction. Some types of sensation and muscle power are lost in neuropraxia. The structure of the nerve is not changed and there is complete, spontaneous recovery after a few weeks. Neurapraxia occurs due to segmental demyelination by mechanical pressure.\\n\\nCrutch palsy occurs when the radial nerve is compressed in the arm from direct pressure against a firm object (crutch). Other examples of neuropraxia include\xa0Saturday night palsy\xa0and tourniquet palsy.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Neurotmesis occurs due to the damage in both axon and nerve by a complete section or scarring of a segment in open fractures. A surgical nerve repair is required since spontaneous recovery doesn\'t take place.\\n\\nOption B: Axonotmesis occurs due to the damage of axons with preservation of the internal architecture of the nerve. It is seen typically after closed fractures and dislocations. Spontaneous recovery can occur but may take a long time.\\n\\nOption C: Neurolysis is the surgical removal of fibrotic tissue inside a nerve trunk.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":9,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient presented with abdominal pain and sterile pyuria; the obtained x-ray is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Putty kidney","Nephrocalcinosis","Staghorn calculus","Psoas calcification"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Putty kidney\\n\\nThe given image shows a homogenous and ground-glass-like calcification of the kidney, appearing like putty. In a patient with\xa0abdominal pain with sterile pyuria,\xa0the given image finding points towards a diagnosis of\xa0putty kidney.\\n\\nThe putty kidney is a characteristic finding of genitourinary tuberculosis (TB), specifically renal TB.\xa0It is caused by the dissemination of the organism through the bloodstream and is, thus, always a secondary TB. There is either reinfection or reactivation of old TB.\\n\\nGenitourinary TB is a cause of sterile pyuria. Diagnosis is confirmed by a tuberculin test or examination of at least 3 consecutive early-morning specimens of urine for acid-fast bacilli. The isolated bacilli are stained with a Ziehl-Neelsen stain and subsequently cultured.\\n\\nCT is the most sensitive modality and the preferred means of visualizing renal calcifications. CT urography is done to assess the various stages of renal TB and its manifestations, which are as follows:\\n\\n• Early: Papillary necrosis\\n• Progressive:\\n\\n• Multifocal strictures.\\n• Generalized or focal hydronephrosis.\\n• Mural thickening and enhancement.\\n• Poorly enhancing renal parenchyma.\\n\\n• End-stage:\\n\\n• Progressive hydronephrosis.\\n• Amorphous dystrophic calcification that involves the entire kidney - putty kidney\\n\\nTreatment involves short-course therapy with antitubercular drugs like pyrazinamide, isoniazid, and rifampicin. Partial nephrectomy, nephrectomy, or reconstructive surgery is often done.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Nephrocalcinosis is the deposition of calcium in the kidney parenchyma or tubules, which is seen in hypercalcemia conditions like hyperparathyroidism.\\n\\nOption C: Staghorn calculus usually occurs in association with urinary infection with Proteus, Pseudomonas, and Staphylococcus and is not a cause for sterile pyuria.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Psoas calcification is a muscle calcification and might be seen developing after acute renal failure and rhabdomyolysis.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/3ba1bc26bd4f8d946d76.gif"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4b095a765525c3eead60.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/08d4c2d2641890f9a49c.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/da519c60d26f94519808.png"}]},{"n":10,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 40-year-old male presents to the dermatology clinic with complaints of an itchy skin rash. On examination, there are multiple concentric rings with scaling, as shown in the image below. The patient reports intense itching, particularly within the rings, and mentions recent travel to Southeast Asia. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":["Trichophyton verrucosum","Microsporum ferrugineum","Trichophyton concentricum","Herpes Simplex virus"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Trichophyton concentricum\\n\\nThe clinical scenario and the image show numerous concentric rings indicative of\xa0Tinea imbricata, resulting from infection by Trichophyton concentricum.\\n\\nThe lesion presents as\xa0a scaling ring; centrifugal spread follows, but within the area of central clearing a second wave of scaling soon arises. The process is repeated to give numerous concentric rings.\xa0Pruritus is intense and may lead to lichenification.\xa0Hypopigmentation may accompany the lesions. It is restricted to the natives of southern Asia, the islands of the South Pacific, Guatemala, southern Mexico, and Brazil.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Trichophyton verrucosum: This dermatophyte fungus typically causes infections known as ringworm in cattle, often transmitted to humans upon direct contact with infected animals. It commonly affects farmers and individuals working in close proximity to livestock.\\n\\nOption B: Microsporum ferrugineum: Microsporum species are known to cause various types of tinea infections, such as tinea capitis (scalp ringworm) and tinea corporis (body ringworm).\\n\\nOption D: Herpes Simplex virus: Herpes Simplex virus infections typically manifest as grouped vesicles or ulcers, often preceded by tingling or burning sensations.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/f956e1e5eb2a9d393d19.png"]},{"n":11,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"The most common pattern of involvement in Pott\'s spine is","options":["Posterior","Paradiscal","Central","Anterior"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Paradiscal\\n\\nThe most common pattern of involvement in Pott\'s spine\xa0is the paradiscal type.\\n\\nPott\'s spine\xa0occurs\xa0secondary to a pulmonary or gastrointestinal primary and spreads through the Batson\'s plexus of veins (paravertebral plexus) and most commonly involves the dorso-lumbar region.\\n\\nTypes of vertebral tuberculosis:\\n\\n• Paradiscal - contiguous areas of two adjacent vertebrae and the middle intervening disc are affected. This is due to the high oxygen requirements of tubercular bacteria and the corresponding high blood supply in this region by the segmental vessels.\\n• Central - the body of the vertebra is involved leading to collapse (causing wedging or concertina collapse) of the affected vertebra. This leads to spinal deformities such as kyphosis and gibbus.\\n• Anterior - the anterior part of the body is affected and spreads through the anterior longitudinal ligament.\\n• Posterior - the posterior complex (pedicle, lamina, spinous process, and transverse process) is affected.\\n\\nThe image below shows various parts of the vertebra and patterns of involvement in Pott\'s spine:\\n\\nClinical features\xa0of spinal tuberculosis:\\n\\n• Back\xa0pain,\xa0stiffness, and reduced movements\xa0are the usual early complaints\\n• Cautious\xa0gait\xa0-\xa0a person with spinal tuberculosis will walk carefully with short steps to avoid pain resulting from movement\\n• Pott\'s paraplegia\\n• Cold abscess\\n• Concertina collapse - a type of compression fracture without the involvement of the intervertebral disc\\n• Deformity - kyphus and gibbus (gradually increasing prominence).\\n\\nImages showing concertina collapse and the deformities seen in TB spine.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f8274dbd4f5391faf3a0.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0939e9e198b3fde111ee.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/850ff6b1d4c3ba6707e6.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/59e8ed9dea844f7a98b2.jpg"}]},{"n":12,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"During recovery from anaesthesia, which of the following drugs is used for the reversal of neuromuscular blockade?","options":["Neostigmine","Atropine","Glycopyrrolate","Propofol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Neostigmine\\n\\nNeostigmine is a cholinesterase inhibitor commonly used during reversal of anaesthesia.\\n\\nReversal agents commonly used in clinical practice:\\n\\nCholinesterase inhibitors - Neostigmine (most common), edrophonium, and pyridostigmine.\\nSugammadex, a cyclodextrin, is the first selective relaxant binding agent for rocuronium and vecuronium.\\nL-cysteine causes the inactivation of gantacurium.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B, C:\xa0 Anticholinergic drugs like glycopyrrolate\xa0and atropine are not used for the reversal of neuromuscular blockade. They only\xa0attenuate the undesirable muscarinic effects of anticholinesterase reversal agents.\\n\\nOption D: Propofol is a barbiturate group of drug which is used for induction of anaesthesia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A young male patient is reluctant to talk about his illnesses. He is suspicious that the information will be used to harm him. He does not trust his family either for the same reason. He does not forgive easily and bears grudges. What is the type of personality disorder that he has?","options":["Schizoid personality disorder","Schizotypal personality disorder","Paranoid personality disorder","Histrionic personality disorder"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Paranoid personality disorder\\n\\nThe given history of long-standing suspiciousness, not trusting people, and bearing grudges for insults is suggestive of paranoid personality disorder.\\n\\nParanoid personality disorder, a part of cluster A personality disorders,\xa0is characterized by pervasive\xa0suspiciousness and mistrust in all interpersonal relationships. They are excessively fearful of perceived threats and deceit and often read hidden demeaning or threatening meanings into benign remarks. They are reluctant to confide in others due to the fear that the information they provide will be used against them maliciously.\\n\\nThey tend to be extremely jealous,\xa0hostile, argumentative,\xa0and\xa0defensive. Their paranoia prevents them from establishing any overly-involved relationship. They persistently bear grudges and can get irritable and angry easily. They use the defense mechanism of projection\xa0by displacing their insecurities on others.\\n\\nThey may not be willing to accept that they need psychiatric help. Psychotherapy is the treatment of choice. Care should be taken by the therapist to not behave defensively if accused of making a mistake.\xa0Pharmacotherapy is generally used to deal with anxiety and agitation. Diazepam is used as an anxiolytic while antipsychotics like haloperidol or pimozide may be required to deal with agitation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Individuals with schizoid personality disorder\xa0are usually eccentric and isolated. They neither desire nor enjoy close relationships. They always choose to be alone and lack close friends. They are emotionally cold and appear indifferent to praise or criticism.\\n\\nOption B: Individuals with schizotypal personality disorder\xa0appear odd and strange to surrounding people. They have ideas of reference, odd or magical thinking, inappropriate or constricted affect, and suspiciousness.\\n\\nOption D: Individuals with histrionic personality disorder\xa0have\xa0excessive emotional and attention-seeking behavior. They are uncomfortable in situations where they are not the center of attraction. They also have inappropriate sexually seductive or provocative behavior. They try to use their physical appearance to draw others\' attention.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient was brought to the OPD with the following deformity. Identify the condition.","options":["Genu recurvatum","Windswept deformity","Genu valgum","Genu varus"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Genu valgum\\n\\nThe given X-ray of a child with\xa0abnormally approximated knees,\xa0divergent ankles, and increased intermalleolar distances depicts\xa0genu valgum.\\n\\nA normal knee has 5-7 degrees of physiological outward deviation (valgum). Any increase in angle can result in\xa0Genu valgum\xa0or knock knee\xa0where knees are abnormally approximated and the ankles are divergent.\\n\\nIntermalleolar distance has been used to assess the degree of genu valgum. It is the distance between the medial malleoli in a standing patient with touching medial femoral condyles. Intermalleolar distances greater than 8 cm are considered pathologic.\\n\\nChildren start developing physiologic genu valgum by age 2, and it becomes most prominent between ages 3\xa0to 4. After\xa0that, it typically decreases to a stable, slightly valgus position by age 7 years. Other causes include\\n\\n• Bilateral Genu Valgum:\xa0Physiologic genu valgum, skeletal dysplasia, metabolic bone diseases, osteoarthritis,\xa0and\xa0rheumatoid arthritis.\\n• Unilateral Genu Valgum:\xa0Trauma, tumors, and infection.\\n\\nOsteoarthritis of the knee\\nRheumatoid arthritis of the knee\\n\\nGenu varum more common\\nGenu valgum more common\\n\\nSubchondral sclerosis\\nPeriarticular osteopenia\\n\\nNo soft tissue swelling\\nSoft tissue swelling (pannus)\\n\\nOsteophytes\\nNo osteophytes\\n\\nHard and bony joint\\nSoft, warm, and tender joint\\n\\nRheumatoid factor negative\\nRheumatoid factor positive\\n\\nKnee deformities are:\\n\\n• Genu valgus (valgum)\xa0or knock knee - Knees are abnormally approximated and the ankles are divergent.\\n• Genu varus (varum)\xa0or bow knee -\xa0Knees are abnormally divergent and the ankles are approximated.\\n• Windswept deformity - Abnormal valgus deformity of one knee with varus of the other.\\n• Genu recurvatum - Hyperextension at the knee joint with backward bending.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/f320522ae1c27020929d.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2b89aa9c4956db3ab600.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A mother brings her 2-week-old neonate with lesions over the body and arms. On examination, thin-walled superficial vesicles are noted as shown in the image below. A disorder of the eccrine sweat gland is suspected. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Fox-Fordyce disease","Bromhidrosis","Milia","Miliaria"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Miliaria\\n\\nThe image shows\xa0clear, thin‐walled, superficial vesicles 1–2 mm in diameter resembling drops of water, without any associated erythema. It is suggestive of miliaria,\xa0a\xa0disorder of\xa0eccrine sweat glands.\\n\\nMiliaria is a common acute or subacute skin condition that arises due to the occlusion or disruption of eccrine sweat ducts\xa0in hot humid conditions, resulting in a leakage of sweat into the epidermis (miliaria crystallina and miliaria rubra) or dermis (miliaria profunda).\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Fox-Fordyce disease\xa0or apocrine miliaria is the obliteration of the apocrine duct at the infundibulum.\\n\\nOption B: Bromhidrosis is characterized by foul-smelling sweat usually due to infection of apocrine glands.\\n\\nOption C: Milia, also known as\xa0“milk spots” are pearly opalescent white epidermal\xa0inclusion cysts, located over the face usually. They resolve spontaneously without scarring and require no treatment.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/2f0b5cae730045f0fa6b.png"]},{"n":16,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in a patient with serotonin syndrome?","options":["Tremor","Hyperthermia","Clonus","Hyporeflexia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hyporeflexia\\n\\nPatients on monoamine oxidase\xa0inhibitors (MAOI) + selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)\xa0can develop serotonin syndrome, which is\xa0characterized by fever, clonus, hyperreflexia,\xa0and tremors.\\n\\nSerotonin syndrome\xa0can occur with any drug that increases 5-HT levels (e.g., MAOIs, SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, tramadol, ondansetron, triptans, linezolid, MDMA, and dextromethorphan). Coadministration of these drugs results in increased serotonin and norepinephrine levels, which are responsible for\xa0the\xa0autonomic symptoms seen\xa0in serotonin syndrome.\\n\\nIt is characterized by 3 A’s:\\n\\n• Neuromuscular hyperActivity- clonus, hyperreflexia, hypertonia, tremor, and seizure\\n• Autonomic stimulation - hyperthermia, diaphoresis, diarrhea\\n• Agitation\\n\\nTreatment:\xa0Cyproheptadine\xa0(5-HT2 receptor antagonist).","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 20-year-old man presents with burning pain on passing urine. He reluctantly admits that he had unprotected sexual intercourse 3 days ago. The following examination findings are seen. Which of the following is the likely causative organism?","options":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Treponema palladium","Herpes simplex virus","Neisseria gonorrhoeae"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Neisseria gonorrhoeae\\n\\nThe symptoms of this patient occurring 3 days after unprotected sex point to urethritis caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae, as its incubation period is 1-5 days.\\n\\nThe most common presentation of gonococcal infection in men is acute urethritis with dysuria and profuse purulent\xa0discharge. Pharyngeal and rectal infections can also occur depending on the type of sexual contact.\\n\\nThe primary site of infection in women is the cervix but the urethra, rectum, and pharynx may be involved. Symptoms include excessive vaginal discharge, dysuria, deep dyspareunia, postcoital and intermenstrual bleeding.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: The most common cause of non-gonococcal urethritis (NGU) is Chlamydia, which has an incubation period of 1-3 weeks. It usually presents with dysuria alone, but mucopurulent discharge can occur.\\n\\nOption B: Syphilis\xa0is primarily a sexually transmitted disease caused by\xa0Treponema palladium\xa0but vertical transmission is also possible. It commonly presents as a\xa0hard, painless genital ulcer with induration,\xa0followed by the development of\xa0rubbery\xa0enlargement of\xa0inguinal lymph nodes.\\n\\nOption C: Herpes genitalis is an infection of the genital skin or mucosa with herpes simplex virus HSV 1 and HSV 2. It presents as painful genital ulcers and dysuria. It is usually preceded by a prodrome of general malaise.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/fa92ac9cf2854bff2792.jpg"]},{"n":18,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Identify the mask in the image used for patients with COVID-19 infection.","options":["Venturi mask","Hudson mask","Nebuliser","Non-rebreathing mask"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Non-rebreathing mask\\n\\nThe mask in the image is a\xa0non-rebreathing mask\xa0(NRBM). It can be\xa0used for patients with COVID-19 infection.\\n\\nA NRBM has a\xa0reservoir bag attached to the mask in order to provide additional reservoir volume. In addition, there is a valve attached, which provides a unidirectional flow of gases. Flutter valves on the side ports prevent room air entrainment, while a valve incorporated between the mask and the reservoir bag prevents the entry of the exhaled gases.\\n\\nNRBM can provide the highest FiO2\xa0of 0.9 to 1.0. without intubation at the fresh gas flow of 12-15 L/min. They are used for preoxygenation before intubation and post-extubation in covid patients.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0The venturi mask is a high-flow, fixed-performance device that\xa0delivers a specific oxygen concentration to the patient.\xa0It is a color-coded device that connects to the oxygen tubing distally and has a nozzle connecting to a mask proximally. Oxygen flows through a central constriction inside the device. Room air enters through surrounding apertures which dilutes the oxygen to the required concentration. The venturi mask is based on Bernoulli\'s principle.\\n\\nOption B: Hudson mask is a simple face mask that delivers oxygen without forming a tight seal against the patient\'s face. There are holes on either side of Hudson\'s mask. This provides air entrainment, meeting the peak inspiratory flow and venting out the expired gases.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0A nebulizer is used to deliver drugs to the breathing system. It generates an aerosol, producing droplets of varying sizes. It is shown below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/2fa13fcb4a43c23ebde0.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a6c141462373599c1de7.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/213c33c11abfa73a7c9b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/729d649aa3d75c64d0d9.jpg"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"The empty delta sign is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":["Subdural hemorrhage","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Cerebral venous thrombosis","Intraparenchymal hemorrhage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cerebral venous thrombosis\\n\\nThe\xa0empty delta sign\xa0is a CT sign of cerebral venous thrombosis.\\n\\nThe \'empty delta\' sign\xa0is a CT sign of\xa0dural venous sinus thrombosis\xa0of the\xa0superior sagittal sinus, where the thrombus becomes less dense and the contrast outlines a\xa0triangular filling defect\xa0(clot) within the sinus lumen. It is only described with\xa0contrast-enhanced CT-scan\xa0(CECT) or MRI with contrast (not with non-contrast CT(NCCT) or non-contrast MRI).\\n\\nOn doing an\xa0NCCT scan, an acutely thrombosed sinus appears hyperdense and expanded. This is called the \'delta sign\'.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/036e112bf2fd20641e73.jpg"}]},{"n":20,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Which of the following statements are true about eutectic mixture of local anesthetic (EMLA)?","options":["It provides analgesia up to a depth of 2 mm","Methohemoglobinemia is a side effect","It is an equal mixture","It is made of Lignocaine and ropivacaine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Methohemoglobinemia is a side effect\\n\\nThe true statement about EMLA(eutectic mixture of local anesthetic) is methohemoglobinemia is a side effect .\\n\\nEMLA (Eutectic Mixture of Local Anaesthetic) is a topical mixture containing prilocaine. The risk of methemoglobinemia is increased in neonates with metabolic disorders or in those receiving drugs that can impair the reduction of methemoglobin. Methemoglobinemia can develop when high doses of prilocaine are administered, typically over 600 mg.\\n\\nIt is an eutectic(in fixed proportions) mixture, not necessarily equal (Option C). It consists of a 1:1 mixture of 2.5% lidocaine (Option A) and 2.5% prilocaine, not ropivacaine (Option D)\\n\\nEMLA can provide\xa0adequate dermal analgesia\xa0for procedures like:\\n\\n• Split skin-graft harvesting\\n• Venipuncture\\n• Intravenous cannulation in children\\n• Laser skin treatments\\n• Lithotripsy\\n• Circumcision\\n\\nEMLA cream\xa0should not\xa0be\xa0used\xa0on\xa0mucous membranes, broken skin, infants younger than 1 month of age, or patients with a predisposition to methemoglobinemia. EMLA is applied 45-60 minutes before the procedure with an occlusive dressing. It provides analgesia up to a depth of 5 mm (Option A) that\xa0lasts for 1-2 hours.\\n\\nOther side effects include- blanching\xa0of the skin,\xa0erythema,\xa0and\xa0oedema.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/120d8cbaaaf49fcb2d8a.png"]},{"n":21,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"On being asked \\"Have you had any trouble sleeping lately?\\" the patient replies \\"I usually sleep in my bed, but now I\'m sleeping on the sofa.\\" What is this type of response known as?","options":["Tangentiality","Derailment","Thought blocking","Circumstantiality"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Tangentiality\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis in this patient is tangentiality. The patient has given a reply that is appropriate to the general topic but does not answer the question exactly; it is tangential. The speech moves away from the actual answer.\\n\\nTangentiality is a formal thought disorder.\\n\\nSymptom\\nDescription\\n\\nCircumstantiality\\nOverinclusion of trivial or irrelevant details that impede the sense of getting to the point\\n\\nClang associations\\nThoughts are associated by the sound of words rather than by their meaning (e.g., through rhyming or assonance)\\n\\nDerailment\\n\\n(loose associations)\\n\\nA breakdown in both the logical connection between ideas and the overall sense of goal directedness. The words make sentences, but the sentences do not make sense.\\n\\nFlight of ideas\\nA succession of multiple associations so that thoughts seem to move abruptly from idea to idea; often (but not invariably) expressed through rapid, pressured speech.\\n\\nNeologism\\nThe invention of new words or phrases or the use of conventional words in idiosyncratic ways.\\n\\nPerseveration\\nRepetition of out of context words, phrases, or ideas.\\n\\nTangentiality\\nIn response to a question, the patient gives a reply that is appropriate to the general topic without actually answering the question\\n\\nThought blocking\\nA sudden disruption of thought or a break in the flow of ideas.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"An 11-year old boy presented with pain and swelling around his knee. His radiograph is as shown below. Biopsy revealed blood-filled cystic spaces separated by fibrous septae. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Aneursymal bone cyst","Osteoclastoma","Osteochondroma","Simple bone cyst"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Aneursymal bone cyst\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario of an 11-year old boy with pain and swelling and radiograph findings showing\xa0lytic lesion of the metaphysis of the upper tibia and biopsy showing blood-filled cystic spaces leads to the diagnosis of an aneurysmal bone cyst.\\n\\nAn aneurysmal bone cyst is a benign, expansile lytic lesion of the metaphysis of the long bones comprising blood-filled cystic spaces. It is more common between the age group of 5-20 years with equal male: female distribution. They occur more commonly in the metaphysis of the long bones like the femur, tibia, and humerus. It may occur on the posterior elements of the spine.\\n\\nPatients usually present with pain and swelling. In cases of spinal involvement, neurologic manifestations can be seen due to nerve root impingement and neurological impairment.\\n\\nDiagnosis:\\n\\nA plain radiograph shows an expansile, lytic lesion in the metaphysis. MRI reveals a typical cystic lesion and numerous intralesional septations along with fluid-fluid levels. A biopsy would reveal blood-filled cystic spaces with fibrous septations which might consist of woven bone.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\nCurettage and bone grafting are done in most cases. Arterial embolization is preferred in cases where curettage is extremely difficult. Low-dose radiation is also effective but avoided in view of potential malignant transformation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B-\xa0Osteoclastoma (giant cell tumor) is a benign but locally aggressive tumor. It usually affects young adults with a peak incidence in the age group of 20-45 years.\xa0A plain radiograph reveals an eccentric, expansile, lobulated lytic lesion in the epiphysis. There is minimal\xa0calcification within the tumor. Biopsy reveals osteoclast-like giant cells scattered among round or spindle-shaped mononuclear cells.\\n\\nBelow is the image showing osteoclastoma:\\n\\nOption C-\xa0Osteochondromas are benign\xa0bone tumors that present commonly in the adolescent age group. Radiograph and MRI would reveal pedunculated\xa0bony protuberance\xa0with a stalk (as shown below) or some lesions with a broad sessile base. Histology shows the cap composed of benign hyaline cartilage covered with a perichondrium, disorganised growth plate and endochondral ossification in the stalk and inner portion of the head.\\n\\nOption D- Simple bone cyst is a unilocular cystic bone cavity that is filled with serous or serosanguinous fluid. It presents most commonly in the\xa0first two decades of life. The most common site is\xa0proximal humeral metaphysis most commonly affecting\xa0metaphysis close to the physis. On the radiograph, a centrally located, lytic lesion\xa0with a well-marginated outline is seen. Histology shows connective tissue lining with foci of reactive bone.\\n\\nWhen a fracture occurs, a fragment is seen in the cavity referred to as the fallen fragment sign\xa0as shown below:","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/fead8c00b1f8dfc8b649.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ea98454b3120144a44e4.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/13024747121d2b39c94a.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/af7c8edd8f9784b1f17b.jpg"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents to you with limitation of abduction and internal rotation of the left hip. The X-ray appearance of his left hip joint is shown in the below image. What is the diagnosis of this patient?","options":["Developmental Dysplasia of hip","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","Ankylosing spondylitis","Perthes disease"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and trethowan\'s sign\xa0seen in the x-ray are suggestive of\xa0slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).\\n\\nThe Klein line is a line along the superior aspect of the femoral neck which normally intersects the epiphysis. In SCFE, Klein\'s line does not intersect the epiphysis and passes superior to it. This is known as Trethowan\'s sign.\\n\\nSlipped femoral capital epiphysis\xa0(SCFE)\xa0occurs during a period of rapid growth with the average age of presentation around 12 years.\\n\\nRisk factors: Obesity, hypothyroidism, growth hormone excess, and chronic renal failure are all-important associations of SCFE.\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Patients with SCFE typically present in the rapid growth phase of adolescence with pain in the thigh, groin, or knee.\\n• On examination, the affected limb is shortened and externally rotated.\\n• There is\xa0a limitation in abduction, flexion, and internal rotation on the affected side.\\n• Flexion of the hip might lead to obligate external rotation and abduction - positive Drehmann’s sign.\\n• Patients may have an antalgic or Trendelenburg gait.\\n\\nOther radiological findings include:\\n\\n• Wide or irregular physis with loss of the anterior head-neck concavity\\n• Sharp metaphyseal border of the head\\n• Loss of total epiphyseal height\\n• Steel sign\xa0(Metaphyseal blanch sign) - The posteriorly displaced femoral epiphysis overlaps over the metaphysis causing\xa0a\xa0crescent-shaped\xa0area of increased density\xa0on the\xa0AP radiograph.\\n• Capener’s sign\xa0–the metaphysis displaces laterally, worsening the slip and causes a\xa0reduction in the double-density\xa0crescent created by the overlap of the posterior acetabulum and proximal metaphysis on the\xa0AP radiograph.\\n• Southwick angle\xa0– The angle between the line drawn perpendicular to the physis and a line parallel to the femoral shaft. As slip worsens, the Southwick angle will increase. It is measured on the\xa0frog lateral X-ray view.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nPerthes disease:\\n\\nIt is characterized by idiopathic avascular necrosis of the proximal femoral epiphysis. This condition commonly affects children of 4-8 years of age.\xa0The child has pain in the hip while performing passive movements. The radiograph shows features of bone necrosis followed by remodeling.\\n\\nDevelopmental Dysplasia of the hip: It\xa0is characterized by\xa0spontaneous dislocation of the hip joint before, during, or shortly after birth. This condition is more common in females. Children with this condition have a waddling gait and on examination abduction and lateral rotation of the hip are decreased. X-ray shows a broken Shenton\'s line and the femoral head lies in the upper and outer quadrants formed by\xa0Hilgenreiner’s line and\xa0Perkin’s line.\\n\\nThe image given below shows the vertical Perkin\'s line and the horizontal\xa0Hilgenreiner\'s line. Normally the femoral head lies in the inner and lower quadrant formed by these lines. But in DDH the femoral head is in the upper and outer quadrant.\\n\\nAnkylosing spondylitis:\xa0It is a chronic progressive inflammatory condition affecting the\xa0sacroiliac joints and the axial skeleton. It is commonly seen in young male adults and they typically present with an insidious onset back pain that improves with exercise. The earliest radiological finding is erosion and fuzziness of the sacroiliac joints and the characteristic late sign is the bamboo-spine appearance.\\n\\nX-ray showing bamboo-spine appearance-","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/e81fe805c7ef9c7ed18d.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ce7f6163cc7dad8cabda.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3f5f0903b96a78fc1c8b.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/82dd4bbd8e5643778ebb.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/66e916f999f8da79f172.png"}]},{"n":24,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"What is the probable diagnosis based on the image of the scan given below?","options":["Pituitary apoplexy","Pituitary macroadenoma","Rathke cleft cyst","Craniopharyngioma"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Craniopharyngioma\\n\\nThe given MRI of the brain demonstrates a predominantly solid enhancing suprasellar mass with a small cystic component\xa0posteriorly suggestive of\xa0craniopharyngioma\\n\\n• It develops from the persistent remnants of the original connection between Rathke\'s pouch and the anterior pituitary gland.\\n• Adamantinomatous (children)\xa0and\xa0Papillary (adults) are two variants of craniopharyngioma.\\n• Calcification\xa0is characteristic of the\xa0adamantinomatous variety\xa0and\xa0predominates in childhood.\\n• These tumours are usually large and heterogeneous, displaying\xa0both solid and cystic components.\\n• The\xa0cysts contain a cholesterol-rich, thick brownish-yellow fluid that is compared to \\"machine oil\\".\\n\\nSymptoms include headache and visual changes\xa0(bitemporal hemianopia) as well as endocrinologic abnormalities such as growth failure and delayed sexual maturation due to\xa0pituitary hypofunction\xa0and GH deficiency.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Pituitary apoplexy is an acute condition caused usually by hemorrhagic necrosis of the pitutary gland.\xa0It shows peripheral enhancement around a non-enhancing infarcted center.\\n\\nOption B: Pitutary macroadenoma show\xa0solid components and demonstrate moderate to bright enhancement.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Rathke cleft cyst are cysts arising from the embryologic remnants of Rathke pouch in the pituitary gland. They show unicameral cysts (without septations) and homogeneous in signal intensity without a solid component.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/83ed19e774a2ca6becf0.jpg"]},{"n":25,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Which of the following induction agents is most appropriate for a patient with cardiac disease?","options":["Ketamine","Etomidate","Propofol","Thiopentone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Etomidate\\n\\nEtomidate is considered to be the most appropriate induction agent for cardiac patients.\\n\\nEtomidate has minimal effect on cardiovascular function due to its lack of effect on the sympathetic nervous system and on the function of the baroreceptors. They act on alpha 2 adrenoreceptors and increase blood pressure which helps to maintain cardiovascular stability after the induction.\\n\\nEtomidate is the induction agent of choice in patients with cardiovascular disease, reactive airway disease, and intracranial hypertension,\\n\\nDue to its unique hemodynamic stability, it is used as a short-term sedative in hemodynamically unstable patients like patients requiring cardioversion or in patients with unstable angina for minor operative procedures.\\n\\nLong-term use of etomidate in trauma or terminally ill patients will cause a reversible inhibition on 11 beta-hydroxylase which decreases cortisol production and increase 11 deoxycorticosterone. This results in\xa0adrenocortical suppression.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- Ketamine\xa0has a stimulatory effect on the sympathetic nervous system and results in increased blood pressure, heart rate, and cardiac output. This leads to an increase in myocardial oxygen consumption and so it is contraindicated in ischemic heart disease.\\n\\nOption C- Propofol is a myocardial depressant agent. It reduces sympathetic activity, leading to vasodilatation that results in a reduction of blood pressure and a decrease in cardiac output.\\n\\nOption D-\xa0Thiopentone\xa0is also a myocardial depressant agent. It causes peripheral vasodilatation, reduction in the ventricular filling, negative inotropic effect, and reduction in a sympathetic outflow from CNS. this results in a reduction in blood pressure and a decrease in cardiac output.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 45-year-old diabetic female with an HbA1c value of 9% requires an emergency fasciotomy in her right leg. Under what ASA PS classification would you place her?","options":["ASA 1","ASA 2","ASA 3","ASA 4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. ASA 3\\n\\nBased on the given clinical scenario, the patient would be placed under ASA 3 grading of ASA PS classification.\\n\\nAmerican Society of Anesthesiologists(ASA) classification is used to define relative risk prior to conscious sedation and surgical anaesthesia. It is simple, reproducible and strongly correlates to perioperative risk.\\n\\nASA grade III includes patients with severe systemic disease that causes significant functional limitations. This category encompasses individuals with one or more moderate to severe conditions, such as poorly controlled diabetes, like in this patient who has a high HbA1c level, or hypertension, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), morbid obesity (BMI ≥40), active hepatitis, alcohol dependence or abuse, an implanted pacemaker, moderate reduction in ejection fraction, end-stage renal disease (ESRD) on regular dialysis, premature infants with a post-conceptual age (PCA) <60 weeks, or a history of myocardial infarction (MI), stroke (CVA), transient ischemic attack (TIA), or coronary artery disease (CAD) with stents placed more than three months ago.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: ASA 1 includes normal healthy, non-smoking, no or minimal alcohol use patients.\\n\\nOption B: ASA 2 includes patients with mild systemic disease without substantive functional limitations. Eg: current smoker, social alcohol drinker, pregnancy.\\n\\nOption D: ASA 4 includes patients with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life. Eg: recent ( < 3 months) MI, CVA, TIA, or CAD/stents, ongoing cardiac ischemia or severe valve dysfunction, severe reduction of ejection fraction, sepsis, DIC, ARD or ESRD not undergoing regularly scheduled dialysis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":27,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"The following waveform when seen on a capnograph suggests?","options":["Malignant hyperthermia","Rebreathing","Tracheal intubation","Esophageal intubation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Malignant hyperthermia\\n\\nThe image in the given question shows a capnograph with a sudden and progressive increase in ETCO2 which is typical of malignant hyperthermia.\\n\\nMalignant hyperthermia is a rare genetic hypermetabolic disease of the muscle. It occurs due to a mutation of the gene for the ryanodine receptor (Ryr 1), located on chromosome 19. It is an ion channel responsible for calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Clinical features include, hypercarbia, hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, arrythmia, hypertension. Dantrolene sodium is the drug of choice. It is given rapidly at a dose of 2.5 mg/ kg intravenously and can be repeated every 5-10 minutes till the attack subsides.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption B: Rebreathing of CO2 would result in failure of the inspired CO2 to return to zero in the capnograph.\\n\\nOption C: Tracheal intubation would show a normal capnograph.\\n\\nOption D: Esophageal intubation would initially detect some CO2 because of swallowed air in the stomach which later returns to zero.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/b0da8fb9ff6445d4be39.jpg"]},{"n":28,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient with obsessive-compulsive disorder picks real or imagined acne lesions predominantly on the face, as given in the image. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Acne excori\xe9e","Acne fulminans","Acne conglobata","Acne mechanica"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Acne excori\xe9e\\n\\nThis given image shows\xa0acn\xe9 excori\xe9e\xa0on the chin of a male with compulsive picking of the face using nail pliers and tweezers.\\n\\nAcn\xe9 excori\xe9e is regarded as a\xa0self‐inflicted\xa0skin condition in which the\xa0sufferer compulsively picks real\xa0or\xa0imagined acne lesions\xa0predominantly on the face. It is mostly seen in adolescent girls.\\n\\nOther psychiatric disorders associated with acne are:\\n\\n• Body dysmorphic disorder -\xa0Some patients have acne as their prime symptom. The perceived acne is out of proportion to physical signs.\\n• Eating disorders -\xa0reported in anorexia nervosa. Acne itself may be a predisposing factor for anorexia in vulnerable teenaged age groups who adopt a diet in an attempt to control their acne.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Acne fulminans or acne maligna is a rare and severe destructive form of acne presenting\xa0primarily in adolescent males. It most frequently affects the\xa0trunk\xa0but can affect the face and presents acutely in association with\xa0systemic symptoms.\\n\\nOptions C:\xa0Acne conglobata represents a\xa0rare\xa0and\xa0severe\xa0form of acne characterized by\xa0multiple\xa0and\xa0extensive inflammatory papules, tender nodules, and abscesses which commonly\xa0coalesce\xa0to form\xa0malodorous draining sinus tracts.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Acne mechanica\xa0describes acne that occurs at the site of\xa0repeated mechanical trauma\xa0and/or\xa0frictional obstruction\xa0of the\xa0pilosebaceous\xa0outlet resulting in comedo formation. Examples include ‘fiddler’s neck’, which may occur on the neck of violin players, and is characterized by well‐defined plaques with the presence of comedones, lichenification, and pigmentation","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/573f47bed88c4936822f.jpg"]},{"n":29,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 25-year-old male was in a major RTA 6 weeks back. Since then he has been experiencing frequent intrusive flashbacks and nightmares about the incident. He avoids the place where the accident occurred. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Adjustment disorder","Acute stress disorder","Persistent complex bereavement","Post traumatic stress disorder"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Post traumatic stress disorder\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with re-experiences (flashbacks and nightmares), avoidance of the stimuli associated with the attacks, and hyperarousal (impaired sleep and anxiety) for more than one month are suggestive of post-traumatic stress disorder(PTSD).\\n\\nPTSD is a psychiatric disorder characterized by increased stress and anxiety occurring after a stressful or traumatic event, (e.g., witnessing or being involved in violent accidents, crime, war, assault, kidnapping, natural disaster, life-threatening illness, physical or sexual abuse).\\n\\nThe predisposing factors for this condition include:\\n\\n• Presence of childhood trauma\\n• Borderline, paranoid, dependent, or antisocial personality disorder traits\\n• Inadequate family or peer support system\\n• Female gender\\n• Genetic susceptibility to psychiatric illness\\n• Recent stressful life changes\\n• Recent excessive intake of alcohol\\n\\nSymptoms of PTSD can be divided into four types:\\n\\n• Intrusion symptoms\\n\\n• Flashbacks- The individual may act and feel as if the traumatic event is reoccurring\\n• Nightmares of the event\\n\\n• Hyperarousal\\n\\n• Increased startle response\\n• Insomnia\\n• Hypervigilance\\n• Difficulty concentrating\\n• Reckless and self-destructive behavior\\n• Irritability and anxiety\\n\\n• Avoidance symptoms:\\n\\n• avoiding memories, thoughts, and feelings of the event\\n• avoiding external reminders of the traumatic event\\n\\n• Negative\xa0cognitions and mood associated with the traumatic event\\n\\n• Inability to remember an important aspect of the traumatic event\\n• Feelings of depersonalization and derealization\\n• Anhedonia\\n• Negative emotional state- fear, horror, anger, guilt, or shame\\n\\nThe duration of these symptoms should be > 1 month to label it as PTSD.\\n\\nPsychotherapies found to be effective for PTSD include exposure therapy, trauma-focused cognitive-behavioral therapy, eye movement desensitization, and abreaction. SSRIs may be prescribed as first-line treatment.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Adjustment disorder is an emotional response to stressful situations like financial issues, medical illnesses, or relationship problems that develop within 3 months of the stressor. The stressor in adjustment disorder can be of any severity. In adjustment disorder, the individual does not experience intrusive symptoms like flashbacks and nightmares, hyperarousal symptoms, and avoidance symptoms.\\n\\nOption B: Acute stress disorder presents with the same symptoms as PTSD, but the symptoms usually occur immediately after the trauma and persist for a period of 3 days to upto 1 month after the trauma.\\n\\nOption C: Persistent complex bereavement disorder occurs after the death of a loved one. Since the death, for 12 months or more, the individual may experience persistent yearning/longing for the deceased, intense sorrow, emotional pain, preoccupation with the deceased, and the circumstances of the death.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":30,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 60-year-old female patient presented with purplish, itchy, flat-topped lesions on his shins for the past 3 months. The patient also gives a history of chewing tobacco for 20 years. She occasionally drinks alcohol and smokes. On examination, the lesions are seen as in the image below. Which of the following is the first line of management?","options":["Ciclosporin","Topical corticosteroids","Methotrexate","Oral corticosteroids"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Topical corticosteroids\\n\\nThe clinical features and characteristic lesions showing fine white lacework in buccal mucosa called Wickham\'s striae shown in the image are diagnostic of lichen planus.\xa0The first line of management is\xa0potent topical corticosteroids like clobetasol propionate ointment 0.05%.\\n\\nLichen planus\xa0is a type of lichenoid disorder affecting the papillary dermis. It is a T-cell mediated autoimmune disease against the basal keratinocytes. Various factors that can precipitate the disease are:\\n\\n• Viruses like Hepatitis B, Hepatitis\xa0C, Human herpesviruses (HHV-6, HHV-8), Varicella zoster\\n• Betel quid which contains areca nut and tobacco leaf\\n• Environmental factors like:\\n\\n• Drugs like antimicrobials, antihypertensives, antimalarials, antidepressants, anticonvulsants\\n• Radiotherapy\\n• Anxiety, depression, stress\\n\\nIt is recommended to avoid spicy and acidic foods, as well as avoid tobacco and alcohol.\\n\\nThe most common presentation of lichen planus is a well-defined area of purple-colored, itchy, flat-topped papules. This description is known as the characteristic \\"6 Ps\\" of lichen planus: planar (flat-topped), purple, polygonal, pruritic, papules, and plaques.\\n\\nWhite streaks\xa0forming a lacework in buccal mucosa are characteristic features and are known as\xa0Wickham’s striae.\\n\\nHistology: Most useful investigation to confirm the diagnosis.\xa0Basal epidermal cell degeneration/destruction by dermal CD8+ T lymphocytes is characteristically seen in\xa0lichen planus. Other findings are:\\n\\n• Hyperkeratosis\\n• The degeneration leads to the formation of spaces in the epidermis known as\xa0Max-Joseph spaces.\\n• Saw-toothed appearance of the Rete ridges\\n• Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate\xa0is present\xa0at the dermo-epidermal junction.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOptions A and C: Ciclosporin, and methotrexate\xa0are used in third-line management and not first-line management of the disease.\\n\\nOption D: Oral corticosteroids are used in the second-line management of the disease.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/2f5f433d4f4d3adba0af.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/66b32808b0bad94828ef.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/73b6717399e22ea62a8e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0b3cce310ca1ebfaa8de.gif"}]},{"n":31,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 30-year-old male patient comes with chronic right shoulder pain. While examining the patient, the following test elicits weakness in the right shoulder. Which of the following muscles/tendons is implicated here?","options":["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Subscapularis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Supraspinatus\\n\\nThe test depicted in the image is the empty can test and the muscle/tendon implicated here is the supraspinatus.\\n\\nIn this test, the patient is asked to resist the downward force by the examiner, when the arms are in the position of 90\xb0 abduction, 30\xb0 flexion, and complete internal rotation(as if emptying a can, hence the name of the test). The result is positive if the affected side is weaker than the normal side and indicates a tear in the supraspinatus tendon.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Infraspinatus – Resisted external rotation - The patient flexes elbows to 90 degrees and stands holding his/her arm close to the body. The patient then externally rotates both arms while the examiner applies resistance; lack of power on one side signifies weakness of infraspinatus.\\n\\nOption C: Teres minor cannot be assessed separately. But it can assessed along with infraspinatus as they both perform external rotation of the shoulder joint. ‘Lag sign’ and the ‘Drop sign’ are used for this purpose.\\n\\nThe lag sign is performed by asking the patient to lift his arms slightly away from the body and place them in maximum external rotation. A positive test is elicited when the patient cannot maintain that position on one side and allows the arm to drift into a more neutral position. This suggests a tear of the infraspinatus, teres minor, or supraspinatus.\\n\\nThe drop sign is similar, here the arm is in 90 degrees of abduction, the elbow flexed to 90 degrees\xa0and the arm maximally externally rotated. When the examiner lets go, the patient would normally hold that position, but if the arm ‘drops’, it denotes a positive test\\n\\nOption D: Subscapularis –Lift-off test - The patient places one arm behind his or her back with the dorsum of the hand resting against the mid-lumbar spine. The patient’s hand is lifted off the back and the patient is told to hold it there. The inability to do this signifies subscapularis weakness.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/8685c9d2eb3e500e26d5.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/811fd79d7ec53e3001bd.jpg"}]},{"n":32,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient presents with joint pain which increases in the early morning when he wakes up and decreases with activity. Examination reveals the following picture (given below) along with inflammation of the wrist. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Osteoarthritis","Reactive arthritis","Psoriathic arthritis","Pseudo gout"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Psoriathic arthritis\\n\\nThe condition depicted in the above image with nail pitting and yellowing of the nails\xa0and inflammation of the DIP joints,\xa0point to a diagnosis of psoriatic arthritis.\\n\\nPsoriasis is a papulosquamous disease with joint and nail manifestations apart from the skin. It has silvery scales.\\n\\nThe joint manifestations in psoriasis are referred to as psoriatic arthritis. It is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy and hence it is associated with HLA-B27. It is seen in 5-30% of patients with cutaneous manifestations. Nail involvement increases the risk of concomitant joint involvement.\\n\\nOther features:\\n\\n• Psoriatic nail changes\xa0like yellowing and hypertrophy\\n• Inflammation of the toes-\xa0dactylitis\\n• Shortening of toes-\xa0telescoping\xa0(because of osteolysis)\\n• Severe deformity- suggestive of\xa0arthritis mutilans\\n• Associated heel pain- suggestive of\xa0enthesitis\xa0(pain at the sites of ligament/tendon attachment)\\n\\nThe x-ray shows \'pencil in cup\' deformity due to tapering of phalanges with cupping of proximal parts. Also all the bones of a single digit maybe involved refered to as ray deformity.\\n\\nThe drug of choice for different types of psoriasis is shown in the pearl below.\\n\\nThe most likely diagnosis for this patient\'s joint pain, which worsens in the early morning and improves with activity, along with the given picture showing nail changes and inflammation of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints, is Psoriatic Arthritis.\\n\\nPsoriatic arthritis is a type of inflammatory arthritis associated with psoriasis. It is categorized under the seronegative spondyloarthropathies and is often linked to the presence of HLA-B27. It is seen in 5-30% of patients with cutaneous manifestations. Nail involvement increases the risk of concomitant joint involvement.\\n\\nSome key features associated with psoriatic arthritis:\\n\\n• Psoriatic nail changes\xa0like yellowing and hypertrophy\\n• Inflammation of the toes - dactylitis (sausage appearance).\\n• Shortening of toes - telescoping\xa0(because of osteolysis)\\n• Severe deformity - suggestive of arthritis mutilans\\n• Associated heel pain - suggestive of enthesitis\xa0(pain at the sites of ligament/tendon attachment)\\n• Radiographic findings, including the \\"pencil-in-cup\\" deformity, which results from the tapering of phalanges and cupping of proximal parts.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/0f27bfe92952c02755f4.png"]},{"n":33,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 26-year-old male presented with erythematous plaques covered with silvery scales over the extensor surfaces of both arms. Punctate pitting was noted on examining the nails. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Psoriasis","Lichen planus","Eczema","Pityriasis rosea"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Psoriasis\\n\\nThe presentation of\xa0erythematous plaques with silvery scales over the extensor surfaces of arms and punctate pitting of the nails are suggestive of psoriasis.\\n\\nPsoriasis is a chronic inflammatory condition of the skin with systemic manifestations.\xa0Plaque psoriasis is the most common type.\\n\\n• The peak occurrence is between 16 to 22 years and between 57 to 62 years. They usually present with itching and a\xa0papulosquamous rash.\\n• The lesions are red, and scaly, and have well demarcation from the normal skin.\\n• They can also be encircled by a clear peripheral zone which is called the Woronoff ring.\\n• They commonly appear in the scalp, trunk, and extensor surfaces of the limbs.\\n• Koebner or isomorphic phenomenon-\xa0describes the appearance of new lesions along the site of trauma (linear lesions)\\n\\nNail findings are commonly seen across all types of psoriasis. Patients present with pits, ridges, and grooves of the nail plate. In the case of nail bed involvement\xa0subungual oil drops,\xa0subungual hyperkeratosis,\xa0and distal onycholysis can be seen.\\n\\nThe characteristic histopathological finding in psoriasis consists of multiple neutrophilic foci in the parakeratotic stratum corneum i.e Munro’s microabscesses and\xa0spongiform neutrophils in the spinous layer of the epidermis i.e spongiform pustules of Kogoj.\\n\\nThe topical application of steroids, retinoids, vitamin D, and emollients is the mainstay of management. Phototherapy and oral methotrexate, acitretin, and cyclosporine can also be used.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Lichen planus\xa0is an inflammatory disorder affecting the skin and mucosal membranes.\xa0The lesions of Lichen planus can be described as\xa05Ps —\xa0purple, polygonal, pruritic, plane-topped\xa0(flat), papules and are shown in the image below.\xa0White streaks\xa0forming a lacework in buccal mucosa is a characteristic feature and are known as\xa0Wickham’s striae.\xa0Nail involvement includes\xa0hyperpigmentation,\xa0subungual hyperkeratosis, onycholysis, and\xa0pterygium unguis\xa0due to adhesion between the dorsal nail fold and the nail bed.\\n\\nOption C: Atopic dermatitis or eczema\xa0is a relapsing chronic inflammatory skin disease. Patients present with itchy erythematous papules/ papulovesicular lesions in\xa0flexural\xa0regions as shown below. Nail involvement is in the form of\xa0coarse pitting\xa0and\xa0ridging.\\n\\nOption D: Pityriasis rosea\xa0is an\xa0acute self-limiting papulosquamous\xa0disorder. It is commonly seen in\xa0children\xa0and\xa0young adults.\xa0The\xa0first manifestation\xa0of the disease is usually the appearance of the\xa0herald patch, which is larger and more conspicuous than the lesions of the later eruption covered by fine scales. Later the center tends to clear and assumes a wrinkled, atrophic\xa0appearance and a tawny color, with a\xa0marginal collarette\xa0of scale attached peripherally, with the free edge of the scale internally - called a\xa0Hanging curtain sign.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/e103f27b423343c6d9a0.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b8227397794b2670dd99.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/346885eac4dafeb9abc3.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a6caf8aadfd775bb9e04.jpg"}]},{"n":34,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 50-year-old lady with a known case of hypothyroidism presents with pain and burning of the lateral half of the palm. On examination, her palms appear as shown below and Phalen\'s test is positive. What is the likely structure under which the affected nerve is compressed?","options":["Ligament of Struthers","Bicipital aponeurosis","Guyon\'s canal","Flexor retinaculum"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Flexor retinaculum\\n\\nThe image shows atrophy of thenar eminence as seen in carpal tunnel syndrome. It is due to compression of the median nerve where it passes under the flexor retinaculum in the wrist.\\n\\nPatients present with pain and paresthesia over the distribution of the median nerve. There may be a weakness of thumb opposition. In chronic cases, the thenar eminence is wasted.\\n\\nTinel\'s sign\xa0will be positive. This sign is elicited by\xa0percussing over the median nerve, reproducing symptoms. In\xa0Phalen\'s test,\xa0\xa0the symptoms are reproduced by holding the wrists in full flexion for 60 seconds.\\n\\nCarpal tunnel syndrome can be associated with rheumatoid arthritis, pregnancy, chronic renal failure, acromegaly, hypothyroidism, and gout. The diagnosis is usually made on clinical grounds but nerve conduction studies can confirm the diagnosis.\\n\\nThe following image shows Phalen\'s test.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Ligament of Struthers connects the medial epicondyle and the humerus. It is associated with pronator syndrome due to the involvement of the median nerve and produces similar symptoms as carpal tunnel syndrome. However, Phalen\'s test is negative.\\n\\nOption B: Bicipital aponeurosis is the insertion of the biceps in the cubital fossa.\xa0It is also associated with pronator syndrome due to the involvement of the median nerve. Phalen\'s test is negative.\\n\\nOption C: The ulnar nerve can get compressed while passing through Guyon\'s canal.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4f0b9aa46d82bdfe1954.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/cbc0395adf4151ade0f8.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d25200948a3c639a6c6f.jpg"}]},{"n":35,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 58-year-old male patient is diagnosed with a fracture of the neck of the femur. T- score on a DEXA scan is -3.0. Which of the following drugs used for this condition acts as a sclerostin inhibitor?","options":["Romosozumab","Odanacatib","Denosumab","Pembrolizumab"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Romosozumab\\n\\nA T-score of -3.0 in a DEXA scan, along with a fragility fracture, is suggestive of severe osteoporosis. Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody that acts as a sclerostin inhibitor\xa0indicated in this condition.\\n\\nRadiological osteopenia can be diagnosed from an X-ray where the bone appears less dense than normal. Typical signs are loss of trabecular definition, thinning of cortices, and insufficiency fractures.\\n\\nDEXA (Dual Energy Xray Absorptiometry) is used to assess bone mass density after a fracture due to a trivial fall or as a preventative measure for adults aged over 60. It has two scores: T-score (compares bone density with that of younger population) and Z-score (compares bone density with the same age group).\\n\\nValues of T-scores\\n\\n≥ -1.0\\nNormal bone density\\n\\n-1.0 to -2.5\\nOsteopenia\\n\\n≤ -2.5\\nOsteoporosis\\n\\n≤ -2.5 plus fragility fracture\\nSevere Osteoporosis\\n\\nTreatment options for osteoporosis include:\\n\\n• Bisphosphonates - first-line treatment to reduce postmenopausal osteoporosis. It includes oral drugs such as alendronate, risedronate, and i.v. formulations such as zoledronate (given only once per year). They act by reducing bone resorption and the rate of bone turnover.\\n• Denosumab (Option C) - a monoclonal antibody that acts against the RANK-L which is essential for promoting osteoclast formation. It is given subcutaneously once every 6 months.\\n• Romosozumab -\xa0a monoclonal antibody that acts as a sclerostin inhibitor and has been shown to increase bone formation.\\n• Parathyroid hormone - Teriparatide, a synthetic agent given intermittently at low doses to promote bone formation.\\n• Strontium ranelate - a drug that promotes bone formation and reduces bone turnover but is used in limited areas due to its adverse effects.\\n• Selective estrogen receptor modulators - Raloxifene, a second-line drug used to reduce future risk of fracture reduction in postmenopausal women.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Odanacatib acts as a cathepsin-K inhibitor which is an enzyme in osteoclast that facilitates bone resorption.\\n\\nOption D: Pembrolizumab is a humanized IgG4 monoclonal antibody that acts against PD1 and can be used in the treatment of melanoma and small-cell carcinoma.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":36,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Which of the following radioisotopes is used in a PET-CT scan?","options":["16F-FDG","18F-FDG","Carbon-11 (C-11)","Gallium-68 (Ga-68)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 18F-FDG\\n\\n18F-FDG is the radioisotope used in a PET-CT scan.\\n\\nPositron emission tomography - CT (PET-CT):\\n\\n• PET imaging is based on the detection of an injected positron-emitting radioactive tracer.\\n• It relies on the\xa0coincidence detection of the annihilation photons (γ), which are released when a positron combines with an electron.\\n• A glucose analogue called\xa0FDG (fluorodeoxyglucose)\xa0is used in PET imaging. FDG is taken up by metabolically active cells, where it undergoes phosphorylation to form\xa0FDG-6-phosphate. Unlike glucose, FDG-6-phosphate cannot undergo further metabolism and becomes trapped within the cell. (So, the patient must be\xa0fasted for 4-6 hrs\xa0prior to imaging).\\n• The trapped FDG-6-phosphate is detected by the PET scanner.\\n• Before injecting FDG, the\xa0blood glucose level\xa0should be measured. It should be <\xa0150 mg/dL.\xa0Good blood glucose control is essential as FDG uptake is competitively inhibited by glucose.\\n• Imaging is\xa0started 60 minutes following FDG injection.\\n\\nIndications of PET-CT:\\n\\n• PET-CT\xa0is used in oncology imaging to detect\xa0malignant cells\xa0as they demonstrate\xa0increased glucose metabolism\xa0relative to normal tissues.\\n• It is indicated for the\xa0differentiation\xa0of a\xa0malignant\xa0from a\xa0benign\xa0lesion.\\n• It is indicated in\xa0tumour staging\xa0and evaluation of tumour recurrence.\\n\\nAreas of normal physiological uptake in PET CT:\\n\\n• Brain tissue\\n• Skeletal muscle, especially after strenuous activity, and laryngeal muscles following speech\\n• Myocardium\\n• Gastrointestinal tract, e.g. intestinal wall\\n• Genitourinary tract (FDG is excreted via the renal system and passes into the collecting systems)\\n• Brown fat (FDG accumulates here as glucose and ATP are required for heat production)\\n• Thymus\\n• Bone marrow\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: 16F-FDG (Fluorodeoxyglucose) is a radiolabeled glucose analogue used in Positron Emission Tomography (PET) to assess metabolic activity in tissues. It is particularly useful for detecting cancer, and infections, and monitoring neurological disorders due to its ability to highlight areas with high glucose consumption.\\n\\nOption C: Carbon-11 (C-11) is used for certain types of PET imaging, particularly in neurology and oncology.\\n\\nOption D: Gallium-68 (Ga-68) is used for imaging certain types of neuroendocrine tumours and somatostatin receptor imaging.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":37,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient with a history of fever, myalgia, and severe arthralgia developed pigmentation on the nose after a few days as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Melasma","Melanocytic papillomatous naevi","Actinic keratosis","Chikungunya"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Chikungunya\\n\\nThe given history of fever, myalgia, severe arthralgia, and nose hyperpigmentation (chik sign) points towards a diagnosis of chikungunya.\\n\\nChik sign refers to a central facial pigmentation predominantly on the nose which develops during\xa0the acute phase of the disease. It helps in the retrospective diagnosis of Chikungunya infection.\xa0It\xa0most commonly affects the nose and cheeks. It\xa0appears as the fever subsides.\\n\\nChikungunya fever is due to infection with Chikungunya virus, spread by mosquitos and direct contact between humans. The Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus mosquitoes are the most common vectors.\\n\\nIt presents as an acute onset of fever, arthralgia, and myalgia with other flu symptoms such as headache, loss of appetite, and nausea. A maculopapular rash develops, which is more pronounced on the trunk.\xa0The rash may occasionally progress to petechiae and purpura.\\n\\nDiagnosis is mainly clinical, however, a definitive diagnosis can be made by the detection of IgM antibodies in the serum with ELISA or RT-PCR.\\n\\nTreatment is supportive with acetaminophen and NSAIDs. The rash can be managed with saline compresses and emollients. Persistent non-healing ulcers can be managed by topical or systemic antibiotics. Most of the skin lesions resolve within 2 weeks.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Melasma is a common skin condition causing dark, irregular patches on the face, often triggered by sun exposure, hormonal changes, or pregnancy. It typically affects the cheeks, forehead, and upper lip.\\n\\nThe following image shows the hyperpigmentation of the face seen in melasma.\\n\\nOption B: Melanocytic papillomatous nevi are acquired dermal nevi that are very common. They protrude from the skin surface and may be pigmented or skin-coloured. Upon histological examination, they exhibit nevus cell nests in the dermis. Therapy consists of excision, shave excision, or laser therapy.\\n\\nOption C: Actinic keratosis is a pre-malignant lesion that consists of crusty, scaly patches of skin. It is associated with UV light exposure and is therefore associated with solar damage to the surrounding skin.\\n\\nThe following image shows the scaly patches of skin suggestive of actinic keratosis of the scalp.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/c2a7209605ede574d0c1.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9bd037b0b5cf68334345.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/acc9a5d4f6f5f586fe46.jpg"}]},{"n":38,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Identify the following test.","options":["Skin Prick Test","Patch test","Casoni\'s test","Radioallergosorbent test (RAST)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Patch test\\n\\nThe Patch test\xa0is shown in the image which is used to confirm the diagnosis of Allergic contact dermatitis.\\n\\nThe patch test is used to detect delayed (type IV) hypersensitivity to antigens that come in contact with the skin, by applying the suspected antigen under occlusive patches to the skin. Patches are removed after 48 hours (or sooner if there is itching and burning). The patch site is evaluated 1 hour after removal, on day 2 and on day 4 to detect delayed reactions.\\n\\nSite - Usually the upper back, or lateral aspect of the arm.\\n\\nIndications for patch testing:\\n\\n• Allergic contact dermatitis\\n• Eczematous disorders where contact allergy is suspected\\n• Eczematous disorders not responding to treatment\\n• Chronic hand and foot eczema\\n• Varicose eczema\\n\\nPatch test should be delayed for 4 weeks after sunbathing, and the patches should not be exposed to the sun or any other UV light. Corticosteroids and other immunosuppressive drugs may falsely reduce positive test responses in sensitized patients. They should be stopped before the patch test, if feasible. However, at doses below 15 mg prednisolone, the response is not likely to be affected.\\n\\nThe image below shows the patch test.\\n\\nOption A: Skin prick test involves an intradermal puncture through a drop of allergen placed on the skin.\\n\\nPositive reaction -\xa0an urticarial weal, which is apparent after 15\xa0min - 45 min.\\n\\nBelow image shows skin prick testing.\\n\\nOption C: Casoni\'s test is an immediate Type I hypersensitivity reaction to hydatid fluid to diagnose Echinococcus granulosus infection.\\n\\nThe antigen extracted from the hydatid fluid is sterilised and injected intradermally in one arm. An equal amount of saline is injected in the other arm as control.\\n\\nPositive reaction - a wheal of about 5cm with multiple pseudopodia-like projection appears within 30mins and disappears in an hour. A secondary reaction of edema and induration appears after 8 hours.\\n\\nThe image given below shows an intradermal injection as done in casoni\'s test.\\n\\nOption D: Radioallergosorbent test (RAST) is a blood test used for estimation of specific IgE.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/59e89fa991807f9ef2e3.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5d9a3eb146a1bf9ad81d.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b348ca6e413389d82fe7.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2a36ba66fe2e8fbdf858.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/21835f5b8fce61ddfe0a.jpg"}]},{"n":39,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 70-year-old patient presents with knee pain in the right knee. The inspection of the knee is shown below. The range of movements is painful and restricted along with a crepitus that is felt during movement. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Gout","Pseudogout"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Osteoarthritis\\n\\nThis elderly patient with knee pain and unilateral knee swelling is most likely having\xa0osteoarthritis.\\n\\nOsteoarthritis is the most common cause of chronic knee pain in individuals of age\xa0>45 years. In the early phases, there is episodic joint pain following overactive use of the diseased joint. As the disease progresses, there is continuous pain and may be associated with stiffness of the affected joint. Morning stiffness if present, is usually brief for around 30 minutes.\\n\\nThe joints involved in osteoarthritis are:\\n\\n• Hip and knee joints\\n• Distal and proximal interphalangeal joints (DIP, PIP)\\n• First carpometacarpal joint (base of the thumb)\\n• First metatarsophalangeal joint (MTP)\\n• Cervical and lumbosacral spine\\n\\nOn examination, there is tenderness in the joint. A crepitus is felt on joint movement and the range of movements is painfully restricted. Bouchard\'s and Heberden\'s nodes may be palpated in PIP and DIP respectively.\\n\\nCharacteristic features of osteoarthritis seen on plain X-ray are:\\n\\n• Marginal osteophyte formation\\n• Localized joint space narrowing\\n• Sclerosis of the underlying bone\\n• Subchondral bone cysts\\n\\nThe image below is an X-ray of osteoarthritis showing the presence of osteophytes, narrowing of the joint space (arrow), and subchondral bone sclerosis.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is characterized by symmetric polyarthritis. Its onset is around the age of 45-50 years.\xa0Clinically, patients present with early morning joint pain and stiffness that relieves with activity. It typically starts in small peripheral joints[metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints],\xa0is usually symmetric, and progresses to involve proximal joints if left untreated. Other hallmark features include flexor tendon tenosynovitis, swan neck deformity, boutonniere deformity,\xa0Z-line deformity, and trigger finger.\\n\\nOption C: Gout\xa0is characterized by the deposition of\xa0monosodium urate\xa0crystals\xa0in joints and connective tissue. It is more common in males and it usually presents as monoarthritis that may be acute and episodic or chronic.\xa0Clinical features\xa0include painful,\xa0stiff or swollen joint, deformity of the affected joint (most commonly first metatarsophalangeal joint),\xa0and subcutaneous nodules.\\n\\nThe radiograph is normal in the initial stages while it shows\xa0erosions, punched-out lesions,\xa0and\xa0sclerosis\xa0with preservation of joint space in the late stages.\\n\\nOption D: Pseudogout results from the deposition of calcium pyrophosphate in the joints. It can be associated with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypophosphatasia, and hypomagnesemia.\xa0It presents features similar to acute gout.\\n\\nSynovial fluid analysis under polarized light\xa0shows\\n\\n• Negatively birefringent needle-shaped monosodium urate\xa0crystals in gout\\n• Positive birefringent rhomboid-shaped\xa0calcium pyrophosphate\xa0crystals in pseudogout.\\n\\nThis patient\'s age and history are more suggestive of osteoarthritis.\xa0The knee joint is the most commonly affected in pseudogout\xa0but in this age group,\xa0osteoarthritis is a more common disease compared to\xa0pseudogout.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/dd0bc8d0e4db9dda8b3d.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a03dead6a7ced852f0d0.jpg"}]},{"n":40,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A patient presents with swelling and tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox after sustaining a fall onto an outstretched hand. Identify the cast given below that was used to manage the patient\'s injury.","options":["Glass-holding cast","Colles cast","Minerva cast","Turnbuckle cast"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Glass-holding cast\\n\\nThe cast applied to a patient in the glass-holding position is known as the scaphoid cast/glass-holding cast. It is used to treat scaphoid fractures.\\n\\nTo apply a scaphoid cast, the wrist is held in slight dorsiflexion and the thumb is in abduction and slight flexion, as if a glass is being held between the index finger and thumb. It extends from just below the elbow to just proximal to the metacarpal necks of the digits. On the thumb, the cast extends to just proximal to the interphalangeal joint.\\n\\nScaphoid fracture presents with pain and swelling over the radial aspect of the wrist. A fall on an outstretched hand could result in such a fracture. The\xa0fracture can be confirmed (when an x-ray does not show a fracture) if there is pain\xa0elicited in the anatomical snuff box on the radial border of the wrist.\\n\\nAn oblique x-ray of the wrist should be able to show a scaphoid fracture.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/9ae1ced9c04b6026d5ae.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/af48d67b8c29b72cb129.jpg"}]},{"n":41,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Identify the correct statements about the deterministic effects of radiation.\\n\\n1. It is associated with damage to the DNA of cells.\\n2. The severity of these effects is independent of the dose received.\\n3. Examples include oligospermia, skin erythema, and epilation.\\n4. Below the threshold dose, harmful effects do not occur.\\n5. The fetus is more vulnerable to these effects between the 2nd to 20th week of gestation","options":["1, 2, 3 and 5 are correct","2, 3 and 4 are correct","1, 2 and 4 are correct","3, 4 and 5 are correct"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 3, 4 and 5 are correct\\n\\nStatements 3, 4, and 5 are correct about the deterministic effects of radiation.\\n\\nThe harmful effects of radiation may be classified into two general categories: Stochastic effects and nonstochastic/deterministic effects.\\n\\nNon-stochastic/Deterministic effects:\\n\\nThese effects do not occur below a threshold dose. The severity of the effect increases as the dose increases. These effects are associated with radiation-induced killing or malfunction of cells. Skin erythema, epilation, cataracts, and transient oligospermia are a few examples of nonstochastic/deterministic effects.\\n\\nThe fetus is most vulnerable to deterministic effects of radiation between the 2nd and 20th week of gestation. Usually, no deterministic effects occur at a dose below 100 mGy which is the threshold for most imaging modalities.\\n\\nStochastic effects:\\n\\nStochastic effects of ionizing radiation are chance events associated with damage to the DNA of cells. They may result in cancer or heritable effects. The incidence of these effects is directly proportionate to the dose of radiation in organs or tissues, but the severity of the effect is independent of the dose received. (All or none phenomenon)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":42,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 30-year-old man presented with numerous itchy skin lesions on his chest and back which show characteristic collarette scales. 2 weeks prior to this he had noticed a round, scaly, erythematous lesion as shown in the image given below, on his thigh. Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":["More common in children and young adults","Topical steroids can be used to relieve pruritus","Associated with viruses like HHV- 6 and HHV-7","Remissions and recurrences are common"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Remissions and recurrences are common\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario, with the image showing a herald patch, is suggestive of pityriasis rosea.\xa0It is an acute self-limiting condition, with very low recurrence rates.\\n\\nPityriasis rosea/pityriasis circinata is an acute self-limiting papulosquamous disorder. It is commonly seen in children and young adults.\\n\\nEven though the exact cause is unknown, T-cell mediated immunity and infectious etiology are thought to be responsible for this condition. An association with infectious agents like human herpesvirus-6 (HHV-6) and human herpesvirus-7 (HHV-7) has been found recently.\\n\\nClinical features include:\\n\\n• Prodrome of a sore throat, gastrointestinal disturbance, fever or arthralgia.\\n• Herald patch\xa0- Large and conspicuous eruptions on the thigh, upper arm, trunk or neck covered by\xa0fine scales\\n\\nThe image given below shows a herald patch.\\n\\n• After 5-15 days, it turns into discrete oval lesions, dull pink in color and covered by fine dry\xa0silvery-grey scales.\\n• These secondary lesions occur as macules and papules that are elliptical or ovular, symmetric, and commonly involve the thorax, back,\xa0and abdomen.\\n• It is followed by a\xa0marginal collarette of scale\xa0with a central wrinkling and atrophy. The collarettes of the scales are attached peripherally. It is also called a\xa0hanging curtain sign.\\n\\nThe image given below shows collarette scales.\\n\\n• The long axes of the lesions characteristically follow the lines of skin tension parallel to the ribs in a\xa0Christmas tree pattern\xa0on the upper chest and back.\\n\\nThe images given below show a Christmas tree pattern along the lines of skin tension on the back and the chest.\\n\\nThe rashes of pityriasis rosea usually resolve by 8 weeks.\\n\\nDermatoscopy shows:\\n\\n• Yellowish background-color\\n• Peripheral arrangement of the scales\\n• Patchy distribution of loosely arranged dotted vessels\\n\\nHistopathology shows:\\n\\n• Superficial perivascular dermatitis with focal parakeratosis, hyperplasia and spongiosis in the epidermis.\\n• Extravasated red blood cells along with a perivascular infiltrate\xa0and subcorneal pustules\xa0are seen in the dermis\\n\\nNo treatment is required for asymptomatic cases. Application of irritants to the skin and tanning should be avoided, since it may result in pigmentary changes. Emollients and antihistaminics are useful to relieve the itching. If symptomatic, various agents are used like:\\n\\n• First-line agents: If severe pruritus or poor cosmetic appeal is present\\n\\n• Topical steroids\\n• Narrowband UV-B therapy\\n\\n• Second-line agents: For a rapid resolution of the lesions, in view of the probable infectious aetiology.\\n\\n• Antivirals like acyclovir\\n• Macrolides like erythromycin\\n\\nPityriasis rosea within the first few weeks of gestation may cause premature delivery and fetal demise. Pityriasis rosea-like eruptions were reported after ingestion of drugs like\xa0gold, captopril, aspirin, metronidazole and clonidine and after vaccinations like BCG, influenza, diphtheria, hepatitis B, and\xa0Pneumococcus.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/085fac3bbb2de9f1eb04.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7d1ff00a126335f41aee.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/211140a81924ceb84568.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/58864982e4a1d7798d29.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c2c26411d3dcb1b568c1.jpg"}]},{"n":43,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 6-year-old child with ventricular septal defect is posted for corrective surgery. Which of the following is the inhalational agent of choice for induction in this procedure?","options":["Desflurane","Sevoflurane","Isoflurane","Halothane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sevoflurane\\n\\nIn the given clinical\xa0scenario of a child undergoing cardiac surgery,\xa0sevoflurane is the inhalational agent of choice for induction.\\n\\nHalothane and sevoflurane are non-pungent and are less likely to irritate the airway or cause breath-holding or laryngospasm during induction. Hence, halothane and sevoflurane are most suited for use in induction among pediatric patients.\\n\\nSevoflurane is less frequently associated with significant myocardial depression, bradycardia,\xa0and arrhythmias\xa0which\xa0are seen with halothane. Hence, sevoflurane is preferred over halothane, especially in all cardiovascular surgeries in the pediatric age group.(Option D)\\n\\nHalothane sensitizes the heart to catecholamines, therefore careful reduction in the epinephrine dose in local anesthetic solutions must be followed.\\n\\nFollowing induction, isoflurane and sevoflurane are the most commonly used inhalation agents for maintenance.\\n\\nOptions A & C- Desflurane and isoflurane\xa0are pungent compounds that irritate the airway and may lead to increased airway resistance, breath-holding, and laryngospasm which make them a poor choice for induction.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":44,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Among the following, which isotope is used in nuclear scanning?","options":["Tc 99m","Cs 137","I 125","Ir 192"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Tc 99m\\n\\nTechnetium agents based on technetium-99m (Tc-99m) radioisotope are the most frequently used agents in nuclear imaging.\\n\\nThe radioactive technetium can be chelated to a number of different compounds to create specific radiopharmaceuticals and optimize imaging of various structures.\xa0The half-life of Tc 99m is 6 hours.\xa0It produces\xa0gamma rays of 140 keV.\\n\\nIodine 125 (I 125), caesium 137 (Cs 137), and iridium 192 (Ir 192) are used in radiotherapy.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":45,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"In which procedure is the instrument shown in the image most commonly used?","options":["Epidural anesthesia","Combined spinal epidural anesthesia","Spinal anesthesia","Bone marrow biopsy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Spinal anesthesia\\n\\nThe instrument shown is the Quincke Needle used in spinal anesthesia. The Quincke needle is a cutting needle with end injection.\\n\\nSpinal needles are long thin needles that are classified based on their tip into:\\n\\n• Cutting needles: Quincke-Babcock, Pitkin\\n• Pencil tip needles: Whitacre and Sprotte\\n\\nTips of various spinal needles:\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : The image below shows the Jamshidi needle used in bone marrow biopsy.\\n\\nOption b : Tuohy needle used in epidural anesthesia is shown in the image below.\\n\\nOption d : Combined spinal epidural (CSE) needle consists of a spinal needle passed through the epidural needle as shown in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/7612445ff0bb36b68544.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0b408c8329ce27ec33dd.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/8f9b69590923dba78cf3.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e8e2b55c11c5ecc11c75.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/277f1773cd45161f43ce.jpg"}]},{"n":46,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 26-year-old woman appears uninterested in taking care of her 5-week-old baby. Her sleep is disturbed and she feels fatigued. She is irritable and complains of having suicidal thoughts. The most likely diagnosis is?","options":["Postpartum depression","Postpartum blues","Postpartum psychosis","Postpartum delirium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Postpartum depression\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario of a postpartum female with complaints of disturbed sleep, fatigue, and suicidal thoughts is suggestive of postpartum depression.\\n\\nPostpartum depression\xa0is characterized by a depressed mood, excessive anxiety, insomnia, anhedonia, and a change in weight, usually within the first 12 weeks postpartum.\\n\\nThere may be associated thoughts of suicide or of harming the baby, which is not present in postpartum blues.\\n\\nA\xa0minimum of 5 symptoms\xa0of major depressive disorder (SIGECAPS - Sleep, interest, guilt, energy, concentration, appetite, psychomotor activity, and suicidal ideation) along with anhedonia must be present.\\n\\nSymptoms should\xa0persist for > 2 weeks\xa0to make the diagnosis of\xa0post-partum depression.\\n\\nDisturbed sleep and fatigue, are common physiological postpartum findings.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Postpartum blues typically develop within one week of delivery. Symptoms include changes in mood, sudden crying outbursts, and a\xa0lack of interest in the baby.\\n\\nOption C: Post-partum psychosis is a psychiatric emergency and usually has a mean time of onset is 2-3 weeks postpartum. The predominant symptoms are hallucinations, delusions, suicidal thoughts, and thoughts of harming the baby. The onset of psychotic symptoms is usually preceded by signs such as insomnia, restlessness, labile mood, and agitation. Lithium, often in combination with an antidepressant, is the treatment of choice.\\n\\nOption D: Episodes of\xa0delirium in the postpartum period are characterized by a fluctuating level of awareness or attention. In this patient, there is no altered sense of awareness.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":47,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 40-year-old man presents with complaints of excess uncontrollable worry over daily life activities, finances, and his health for the past 2 years. Because of this, he feels restless most of the time. He also complains of fatigue and disturbed sleep. Which of the following screening tools is useful for identifying this patient’s condition?","options":["PHQ-9","CHAT","GAD-7","CAM"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. GAD-7\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of a generalised anxiety disorder(GAD). The\xa0GAD-7 scale is used in the assessment of generalized anxiety disorder.\\n\\nWhen assessing for GAD, clinical professionals look for the following according to the DSM-5 (and ICD-11) criteria:\\n\\n• The presence of excessive anxiety and worry about a variety of topics, events, or activities.\\n• Worry occurs more often than not for at least six months and is clearly excessive.\\n• The worry is experienced as very challenging to control.\\n• The worry in both adults and children may easily shift from one topic to another.\\n• The anxiety and worry are accompanied by at least three of the following physical or cognitive symptoms (In children, only one of these symptoms is necessary for a diagnosis of GAD):\\n• Edginess or restlessness\\n• Tiring easily; more fatigued than usual Impaired concentration or feeling as though the mind goes blank\\n• Irritability (which may or may not be observable to others)\\n• Increased muscle aches or soreness\\n• Difficulty sleeping (due to trouble falling asleep or staying asleep, restlessness at night, or unsatisfying sleep)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Patient Health Questionnaire 9 (PHQ-9) is a tool used to assess depressive symptoms in patients with major depressive disorder.\\n\\nOption B: CHAT checklist is used in autism (CHecklist for Autism in Toddlers).\\n\\nOption D: Confusion assessment method (CAM) is a bedside screening test used in the diagnosis of delirium.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":48,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"A 39-year-old man reported that he washes his hands 15-20 times a day, to the point of it taking a toll on his workplace performance. He says this is due to repetitive thoughts that his hands are contaminated with dirt. What is the most probable diagnosis in this case?","options":["Bipolar disorder","Obsessive compulsive disorder","Schizophrenia","Mania"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Obsessive compulsive disorder\\n\\nThe man is suffering from\xa0obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), as evidenced by the obsession (doubt that his hands are contaminated), followed by a compulsion (washing his hands way too many times). An obsession with contamination is a common symptom of adult OCD.\\n\\nObsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) comprises symptoms that include intrusive thoughts, rituals, preoccupations, and compulsions. These recurrent obsessions or compulsions cause severe distress to the patient. The patient might have an obsession, a compulsion, or both.\\n\\nThe recurrent and intrusive thoughts, feelings, ideas, and sensations are referred to as obsessions. A compulsion is a conscious, standardized, recurrent behavior, eg: counting, and checking, and is done in order to reduce the anxiety associated with the obsession.\\n\\nTreatment of OCD includes:\\n\\n• Pharmacotherapy:\xa0SSRIs or clomipramine\\n• Behavioural therapy: Approaches include:\\n\\n• exposure and response prevention\\n• desensitization\\n• thought stopping\\n• flooding\\n• implosion therapy\\n• aversive conditioning\\n\\nA combination of behavioural and pharmacotherapy is most effective.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder characterized by alternating episodes of mania or hypomania and depression. It can affect daily functioning, with symptoms ranging from elevated energy and impulsivity to sadness and fatigue.\\n\\nOption C: Schizophrenia is a chronic mental disorder characterized by hallucinations, delusions, disorganized thinking, and impaired functioning. It typically manifests in early adulthood and affects perception, emotion, and behaviour.\\n\\nOption D: Mania is a state of elevated mood, increased energy, and hyperactivity, often seen in bipolar disorder. It can lead to impulsivity, reduced need for sleep, and impaired judgment.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":49,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"What is the most likely diagnosis for a patient presenting with a bone tumor showing a sunburst appearance on imaging?","options":["Osteoclastoma","Osteoid Osteoma","Osteosarcoma","Ewing\'s Sarcoma"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Osteosarcoma\\n\\nA bone tumor with a sunburst appearance on imaging is most likely osteosarcoma.\\n\\nOsteosarcoma is the most common non-hematologic primary malignancy of bone. It occurs most commonly in the second decade of life. It is typically metaphyseal. The distal femur, proximal tibia, and proximal humerus are the commonly involved sites.\\n\\nIn osteosarcoma, the tumor lifts the periosteum and incites an intense periosteal reaction. The periosteal reaction is irregular and it may take the form of a Codman\'s triangle or it may have a sunburst (sunray) appearance on X-ray.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Osteoclastoma or Giant cell tumor is commonly seen in the age group of 20-40 years with a slight female preponderance. The distal end of the femur is the most common location followed by the proximal tibia and distal radius.\\n\\nRadiographic findings:\\n\\n• Eccentrically located solitary lytic lesion in the epiphysis\\n• Soap bubble appearance\\n• Periosteal new bone formation is absent.\\n\\nOption B: Osteoid Osteoma\\n\\nThe image shows presence of metadiaphyseal cortical osteoid osteoma involving the upper end of the femur with radiolucent nidus surrounded by cortical sclerosis.\\n\\nNidus of osteoid osteoma is present in the cortex and hence CT scan will be the best investigation to diagnose osteoid osteoma.\\n\\nImage:\\n\\n• Black arrow - Nidus\\n• White arrow - Cortical sclerosis\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Ewing\'s sarcoma is a primary malignant tumor of the bone commonly involving the age group of 4-25 years involving the diaphysis of the long bones like the femur, tibia, fibula, and humerus.\\n\\nX-ray shows onion-peel appearance due to deposition of periosteal reaction in layers is the typical finding.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a90f2e9c18905229d991.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e25fdc9110051f3aae20.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/8283612cf40dff2e27ae.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4912c6aecb8cd282d6fd.jpg"}]},{"n":50,"subject":"Minor Subjects","text":"Identify the segment marked in the image below.","options":["V","VII","IVa","III"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. V\\n\\nThe liver segment highlighted in green in this CT section is\xa0segment V. The section of the CT given is taken below the portal vein as the cross-section of the portal vein before its bifurcation can be viewed.\\n\\nA horizontal plane known as the portal plane divides the liver where the\xa0portal vein\xa0bifurcates and becomes horizontal. This divides each section of the liver into superior and inferior segments:\\n\\n• Left lateral section: segment II above and segment III below the portal plane\\n• Left medial section: segment IVa above and segment IVb below the portal plane\\n• Right anterior (or medial) section: segment VIII above and segment V below the portal plane\\n• Right posterior (or lateral)\xa0section: segment VII above and segment VI below the portal plane\\n\\nShown below is a section from below the portal plane.\\n\\nCouinaud classification is the commonly used classification of the liver. It divides the liver is divided into eight segments.\xa0It is based on the distribution of portal venous branches in the liver parenchyma. Each segment has its own vascular supply, biliary drainage, and lymphatic drainage.\\n\\nThe\xa0Couinaud\'s liver segments\xa0are as follows:","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/f9d96a0eac80e4fbd20c.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c04738c58f9b107a651e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3c0a55f73d949f410b2d.jpg"}]}]}')}}]);